00:51:42.100 --> 00:52:00.470
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Parents get their kids on the program when they're under 18. And I guess it shouldn't be surprising, although I was pretty surprised by this-- 2/3 of them stay on disability when they become adults. They stay on a program that penalizes you if you start to do well in school or if you start to work as a grown up.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>父母在孩子18岁以下时让他们进入该项目。我想这不应该令人惊讶，尽管我对此感到非常惊讶——他们中有三分之二在成年后仍然留在残疾项目里。他们留在了一个如果你在学校开始表现出色，或者如果你作为一个成年人开始工作就会惩罚你的项目中。

00:52:00.470 --> 00:52:02.320
<v Talia Mcfadden>I'm kind of scared to work.
<v Talia Mcfadden>我有点害怕工作。

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:03.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You're scared to work.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你害怕工作。

00:52:03.750 --> 00:52:09.640
<v Talia Mcfadden>Yeah, because if I work, they'll go down on my  check.
<v Talia Mcfadden>是的，因为如果我工作，他们就会减少我的支票。

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:31.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Talia  McFadden is 27-years-old. She's been on the disability program most of her life. She suffers from anxiety and depression. When she was 17, she tried to kill herself. The one time Talia did have a very part time job tutoring, Social Security saw she was making some income, so they reduced her check. Talia freaked out when this happened. She quit her job.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>塔莉亚·麦克法登（Talia McFadden）27岁。她一生大部分时间都在领残疾补助。她患有焦虑和抑郁症。17岁时，她试图自杀。塔莉亚唯一一次有一份非常兼职的辅导工作时，社保局看到她在赚取一些收入，所以他们减少了她的支票。塔莉亚在发生这种情况时崩溃了。她辞去了工作。

00:52:31.630 --> 00:52:49.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She says her symptoms come and go, and she's just not confident she could hold down a full time job. So she doesn't want to do anything that can threaten a dependable, secure monthly check. That is incredibly valuable to her. Although, when she talks about her experience working as a tutor, sounds like that was pretty valuable too.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她说她的症状时好时坏，她只是不自信能保住一份全职工作。所以她不想做任何可能威胁到那份可靠、安全的月支票的事情。这对她来说非常有价值。尽管，当她谈论她做辅导员的经历时，听起来那份工作也非常有价值。

00:52:49.530 --> 00:53:07.330
<v Talia Mcfadden>Most of my students said, I trust you. And I felt like-- I felt appreciated, you know. Sometimes the parents would say that the kid wouldn't stop talking about me.
<v Talia Mcfadden>我的大多数学生说，我相信你。我觉得——我觉得自己被赏识了，你知道。有时家长会说孩子不停地谈论我。

00:53:07.330 --> 00:53:10.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So that must have been hard to give that up.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以放弃那份工作一定很难。

00:53:10.790 --> 00:53:18.280
<v Talia Mcfadden>Yeah. Because it kind of gave me a sense of wanting to be and wanting to live.
<v Talia Mcfadden>是的。因为它给了我一种想要存在和想要活着的意义。

00:53:18.280 --> 00:53:21.240
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It gave you a sense of wanting to be and wanting to live?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它给了你一种想要存在和想要活着的意义？

00:53:21.240 --> 00:53:30.790
<v Talia Mcfadden>Yeah, because I just feel like I don't belong. Like I'm not helping to make things better.
<v Talia Mcfadden>是的，因为我只是觉得自己不属于这里。我不觉得自己在帮助让事情变得更好。

00:53:30.790 --> 00:54:13.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The idea that we, as a society, should offer support to disabled children living in poverty, I haven't taken a survey or anything, but I'm guessing a large majority of Americans would be in favor of that in some form. We'd have a hard time agreeing exactly how we want to offer support. But I think there are some basic things we all agree on. Kids should be encouraged to go to school. Kids should want to do well in school. Parents should want their kids to do well in school. Kids should be confident their parents can provide for them, regardless of how they do in school. And the goal should always be for children to become more and more independent as they grow older, and, if possible, support themselves, somewhere around the age of 18.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们作为一个社会应该为贫困的残疾儿童提供支持的想法，我没有做过调查，但我猜绝大多数美国人会以某种形式支持这一点。我们很难就我们想要如何提供支持达成一致。但我认为我们都同意一些基本点：应该鼓励孩子上学。孩子应该想要在学校表现出色。父母应该希望他们的孩子在学校表现出色。孩子应该有信心，无论他们在学校表现如何，他们的父母都能供养他们。目标应该始终是让孩子在长大后变得越来越独立，如果可能的话，在18岁左右能够自食其力。

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:22.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This program stands in opposition to every one of these aims.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这个项目违背了所有这些目标。

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:36.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So somewhere around 30 years ago, the economy started changing in some important ways. There are now millions of Americans who do not have the education or the skills for the current economy. And this does not seem temporary.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以大约30年前，经济开始在一些重要方面发生变化。现在有数百万美国人没有适应当前经济的教育或技能。这似乎不是暂时的。

00:54:36.860 --> 00:54:51.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Politicians certainly pay lip service to this problem during election cycles and such. But American leaders have not sat down and said, look, things have fundamentally changed. What are we going to do for these people? We need a plan.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>政客们在竞选周期中肯定会对这个问题进行口头上的支持。但美国领导人并没有坐下来讨论说：看，事情已经从根本上改变了。我们要为这些人做些什么？我们需要一个计划。

00:54:51.610 --> 00:55:21.510
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In the meantime, the disability programs have become the default plan. Programs that, along with the associated health care, cost about $260 billion a year. And I know this is obvious, but a quarter trillion dollars? That's a lot of money. That's eight times more than we spend on welfare. In fact, it is more than we spend on welfare, food stamps, the school lunch program, and subsidized housing combined. A lot more.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>与此同时，残疾项目成了默认计划。这些项目连同相关的医疗保健，每年花费约2600亿美元。我知道这很明显，但四分之一万亿美元？那是一大笔钱。那是我们花在福利上资金的八倍。事实上，它比我们花在福利、食品券、学校午餐计划和补贴住房上的总和还要多。多得多。

00:55:21.510 --> 00:55:46.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Think for a minute of what we could do with a quarter trillion dollars. We could decide what we want is to expand welfare payments or food stamps. We could decide that the best plan is just to hand out cash to every out of work American. Or we could decide not to spend the money. Let everybody pay less in taxes. There are options. We just never made a decision.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>花一分钟想想我们能用这四分之一万亿美元做什么。我们可以决定我们想要的是扩大福利支付或食品券。我们可以决定最好的计划就是给每个失业的美国人发放现金。或者我们可以决定不花这笔钱。让每个人少交税。有很多选择。我们只是从未做出决定。

00:55:46.290 --> 00:56:28.780
<v Ira Glass>Chana Joffe-Walt. She's with Planet Money, which is a co-production of our program and NPR News. At the Planet Money website, you can see some really startling graphs and charts that tell this story in a way that you really can't tell a story on the radio. Seriously, some of these are kind of amazing. That is at planetmoney.com.
<v Ira Glass>查娜·乔菲-沃尔特。她是Planet Money的成员，这是我们节目和NPR新闻的联合制作。在Planet Money网站上，你可以看到一些非常惊人的图表和数据，它们以一种你在广播里讲故事无法呈现的方式讲述了这个故事。说真的，其中一些令人惊叹。网址是planetmoney.com。

